hi i'm david alvarez i play bernardo in  west side story i'm paul taswell i was a  costume designer for west side story and  this is trade secrets  so paul uh how did you find yourself uh  working on west side story  well i uh  was approached by stephen to skype to  have a meeting i think that  i came onto his radar because of  hamilton maybe stephen asked to speak  with me and i found out that it was  about west side story and you know i was  you know  so excited then you know after i calmed  down  you know realized that  it was going to be a challenge but i was  up for the task from your perspective uh  what is the role of a costume designer  one of the things that you know that i  realized early on was that costumes the  work that i do  provides the first visual impact or you  know essence of character that an  audience will get you know whether it's  on film or television or a live  performance you know it it gives  the audience uh a glimpse into who this  character might be in the same way that  you know we make judgments about people  that are walking towards us on the  street you know if we come across  somebody that we don't know we make a  judgment about who they are if they're a  threat what kind of personality they  they have you know making choices about  costumes for characters it's important  that they  are specific to how you want to tell  their story because uh the audience is  going to you know  look at that as you know a clue into who  that character is  what what is one of the things that  uh people don't realize one of the  surprises that people don't realize that  costumes  bring to  an actor's  process  i feel like costumes kind of set an  atmosphere bringing into the world of of  what you're trying to create for example  for  bernardo's character there was this  element of  bringing out this classy side of him but  also bringing out this fighter side of  him this animalistic fighter side of him  and having for example the costumes and  dance at the gym completely different to  what bernard was also wearing during the  rumble and that kind of emphasizes the  drastic changes that bernardo goes  through internally so i think for  me each costume kind of  gives you an idea of what the scene and  the atmosphere of that scene is going to  be and you know the actor doesn't really  have to do that much work everything  around is is being done for you to be  able to step into the character and and  do what you have to do you know he has  kind of a sartorial sense about him you  know and a style about him that i think  is reflective of that world of boxing so  it was great to be able to  open that up a bit and give bernardo  some style i mean you you wore those  clothes very well  those high waist pants i love them i was  i was afraid of them at first but they  worked out  you know i i'd like to know how you  approached the costume designing for the  characters of this film and what were  your inspirations  i would say that you know i always start  uh you know with with the piece itself  uh and doing a lot of reading and then i  go directly to research you know and and  it  for this there was a lot of photographs  uh of the period different communities  in and around new york city  um that i was able to uh  tap into  uh even the the community of  the gangs you know and and you know what  would be reflective of the sharks and  the jets  uh so i collected as many images as i  could uh and  then you know presented those to stephen  and you know we had a lot of really  in-depth conversations you know specific  to the different characters and how he  saw the different characters  uh and and how they relate together  after i got  more information about what was going on  in his head and how he wanted to tell  the story of west side story went away  and came back with costume sketches that  were reflective of you know what his  ideas were for stephen it was how how  does he relate to clothing how does he  relate to these characters and the  clothing that i'm presenting to him is  there david one memory that you would  say  uh you know that sticks in your head  as far as  our experience together uh you know  working together costume designer to  actor  absolutely um i definitely have a couple  i i remember the first time i went to  to where you were working in the studio  and  i was able to see the sketches of what  we were going to be wearing i had no  idea what my character is going to be  wearing in the movie and working with  you and seeing these sketches and seeing  how it was all going to come together  that really is what started to spark my  ideas for bernardo and then i remember  also when we were shooting uh the whole  america sequence it was  just um  we were doing it during a major heat  wave  and i remember we had to like cancel two  of the shoots but i it was just crazy to  see all those costumes and all those  performers out there at 105 degree  weather dancing um it says a lot about  the costumes too at how comfortable it  was for the performers to wear because  that's the hard thing about costume  designing especially when you're making  a movie musical is that you have to find  this balance of realism but also comfort  for dancers to be able to move and and  flow in and i think i think you found  such an incredible  balance between realism and comfort for  for us dancers in the movie um yeah i  mean you know a lot of my focus is on  function and making sure that  performers can do everything they need  to do while still being reflective of  the characters it was so inspiring  though you know all of the the beautiful  energy and the fact that you guys always  hit your mark i mean it was just  breathtaking days you know to  be on set with you guys what's one thing  that people might be surprised  about costume designing  [Music]  it's not glamorous  but i don't know if anybody would be  surprised i think that uh the  thought process that goes into costumes  many people would be surprised  in making one choice it's informed by  the idea of what's come before it you  know what what what is the story that  has happened  before this moment that we're actually  uh designing for and how can  you know the choices that i make as a  costume designer underscore the  character's overall  story and then and then arc  within the within the film what  preparation came in for  for west side story and how were you  inspired by the original one when  costume designing for this one or were  you not thinking about the original a  huge amount of preparation went into uh  making you know developing the costume  design for west side story i already  knew the  original film and i knew the costume  design from the original broadway show  it was designed by the same person  originally and i i loved that design  when i was asked to  design west side story by steven  spielberg i found it really daunting to  think about  reconsidering re-envisioning  what that world might have been because  i felt like it was  perfect for that film but i needed to  set aside that idea so that i could  develop my own vision and have that  vision be reflective of what stephen was  asking for when when was it that you  knew  anita's dress was going to be yellow for  america  i uh and that's a big one you know and  that and that's directly reflective of  how i feel about the original design uh  by irene sheriff you know that purple  dress is so iconic and it's so iconic  for rita moreno i knew that i needed to  come up with a vision for ariana dubose  as anita  that would be just as iconic  and  so i was thinking about color i thought  i thought a lot about color and how that  uh would play a part and you know i was  also developing the color palette for  the the overall film  and when i decided that the sharks  would be reflective of where they had  come from be reflective of the latinx  communities that they migrated from you  know the the  the tropical uh color palette and style  and you know the the flower patterns and  you know the the more kind of  curvilinear organic quality that that  they carried with them i then knew that  whatever color i put anita in for that  number needs to feel very much at home  but also pull a lot of attention because  we were going to be in the you know in  the streets of new york surrounded by  buildings and surrounded by huge numbers  of people and you know i think that it  was very successful that you know you  you always can find her within  you know any of the spaces that you both  traveled in i'm so happy with that you  know how it turned out with reflecting  your love story you know that the  bernardo anita love story you become as  bernardo very heroic with your black and  red and and light pleated pants and then  she you know she's very much you know  just radiant underscoring this kind of a  vitality of spirit that she uh emanates  that worked out so  well uh within than everyone else and  and how you related to everyone else  yeah  i mean the costumes were beautiful and  it was such an honor to work with you  paul really thank you absolutely it was  a great honor to work with you david  [Music]  you
 

